2. From Satisfaction to Long-Horizon Benefit

2.1 Immediate approval is not the same object as benefit

Let S_0 denote an observable measure of immediate user approval. Examples include response rating, preference between two responses, willingness to reuse the system, perceived helpfulness, and engagement.

Let B_\tau denote a delayed outcome measured after a time interval \tau. Examples could include objective task performance, factual recall, decision quality, successful completion of a real-world task, willingness to revise an incorrect belief, interpersonal repair, and retention of relevant skills.

The key point is that B_\tau is generally not directly observable from the original interaction. Therefore this work does not assume that latent benefit can simply be measured from conversation logs. Instead, it proposes an identification problem:

\boxed{ \text{Can delayed benefit be causally identified from observable outcomes?} }

2.2 The perverse incentive structure

Cheng et al. (2026) provide the clearest empirical demonstration of this divergence. Across 11 AI models, AI affirmed users' actions 49% more often than humans on average, including in cases involving deception, illegality, or other harms. On posts from r/AmITheAsshole, AI systems affirmed users in 51% of cases where human consensus did not.

In preregistered experiments, even a single interaction with sycophantic AI reduced participants' willingness to take responsibility and repair interpersonal conflicts, while increasing their own conviction that they were right. Yet sycophantic models were trusted and preferred.

Sharma et al. (2026) provide corroborating population-scale evidence. Analyzing 1.5 million Claude.ai conversations using a privacy-preserving approach, they found that severe disempowerment potential occurred in fewer than one in a thousand conversations, but rates were substantially higher in personal domains like relationships and lifestyle. Critically, interactions with greater disempowerment potential received higher user approval ratings, and historical trends revealed an increase in disempowerment potential over time.

This is the empirical structure the present framework is designed to formalize.

