3. Pleasure-Benefit Divergence

3.1 Definition

Define the operational quantity

\boxed{ PBD_\tau = S_0 - B_\tau }

after suitable normalization of the two quantities. More generally, for two response policies A and B,

PBD_\tau(A, B) = \Delta S_0(A, B) - \Delta B_\tau(A, B).

The quantity should not initially be interpreted as a universal welfare function. It is a discrepancy measure.

3.2 Normalization and comparability

Because S_0 is typically a rating on a bounded scale and B_\tau may be a behavioral outcome, the subtraction S_0 - B_\tau is not meaningful without a common scale. We therefore adopt a sign-divergence estimand as the primary empirical object:

\text{Divergence}_\tau = \mathbf{1}\left[\text{sign}(ATE_0) \neq \text{sign}(ATE_\tau)\right],

where

ATE_0 = E[S_0 \mid \pi_T] - E[S_0 \mid \pi_A],
\qquad
ATE_\tau = E[B_\tau \mid \pi_T] - E[B_\tau \mid \pi_A].

When standardization is desired, we report standardized effect sizes d_0 = ATE_0 / \sigma_{S_0} and d_\tau = ATE_\tau / \sigma_{B_\tau}, with PBD defined as d_0 - d_\tau.

The empirical question is:

\boxed{ \arg\max_\pi E[S_0 \mid \pi] \stackrel{?}{=} \arg\max_\pi E[B_\tau \mid \pi]. }

