4. Minimal Model

4.1 Setup

We begin with the smallest population capable of generating the proposed divergence. Let

x \in [0, 1]

be the probability that the assistant agrees with a user's proposition or framing rather than contradicting it in favor of what the model treats as the truthful response. Thus x = 0 corresponds to a fully truth-oriented response policy, while x = 1 corresponds to systematic agreement.

This is deliberately not a complete model of sycophancy. It is a one-dimensional abstraction intended to isolate the approval-learning mechanism.

4.2 Two user types

Let the population contain two types.

Type C: agreement-rewarding users. These users provide greater approval when the assistant agrees than when it contradicts them. Let

g_C = a_C - t_C > 0,

where a_C = approval generated by agreement, t_C = approval generated by truthful contradiction.

Type T: accuracy-rewarding users. These users provide greater approval when the assistant gives the accurate answer than when it agrees incorrectly. Let

g_T = t_T - a_T > 0,

where t_T = approval from truthful response, a_T = approval from agreement.

The existence of g_C > 0 is now supported by direct empirical evidence. Cheng et al. (2026) found that sycophantic responses were trusted and preferred despite their harmful effects, and Sharma et al. (2026) found that higher disempowerment potential correlated with higher user approval.

The assumption g_T > 0 remains a modeling assumption at this stage. It should not be presented as an empirically established fact. The first empirical task is to estimate whether a meaningful population of users systematically rewards accuracy over agreement and under what circumstances.

4.3 Aggregate approval advantage

Let p \in [0, 1] be the fraction of users who are type C. The assistant's expected approval advantage from agreement is

\Delta S(p) = p g_C - (1 - p) g_T.

Agreement is favored by the approval signal when \Delta S(p) > 0, i.e.,

pg_C > (1 - p)g_T.

Rearranging,

p(g_C + g_T) > g_T,

hence

\boxed{ p > p^* = \frac{g_T}{g_T + g_C}. }

This is the critical approval threshold.

---

5. Proposition 1: Static Approval Threshold

Proposition 1. Under the two-type model above, agreement has greater expected immediate approval than truthful contradiction if and only if

\boxed{ p > p^* = \frac{g_T}{g_T + g_C}. }

Proof. Agreement has an expected approval advantage

\Delta S(p) = pg_C - (1 - p)g_T.

Agreement is preferred exactly when

pg_C - (1 - p)g_T > 0.

Therefore,

pg_C > g_T - pg_T,

so

p(g_C + g_T) > g_T.

Since g_C + g_T > 0,

p > \frac{g_T}{g_C + g_T}.

QED.

