6. Continuous Preference Extension

6.1 The limitation of binary types

The two-type model treats users as discretely either agreement-rewarding or accuracy-rewarding. This is a tractable first approximation, but it cannot represent the empirically observed continuity of preference strength.

Chidambaram et al. (AISTATS 2026) prove a fundamental identifiability result: binary comparisons are insufficient for identifying latent user preferences from finite user data and infinite users, while even incomplete rankings over three or more responses ensure identifiability. This means that if the empirical program relies only on pairwise preference elicitation, the latent preference structure is not identifiable. The experimental design must therefore use ternary or higher-arity ranking tasks.

Imai et al. (MixDPO, arXiv 2601.06180) generalize Direct Preference Optimization by modeling preference strength as a latent random variable drawn from a learned distribution, following the mixed logit tradition in behavioral economics. Their key insight is that a fixed sensitivity parameter couples frequency with influence: frequently occurring but weakly expressed preferences can outweigh infrequent but strongly expressed ones, causing aggregate improvements to mask regressions on underrepresented groups.

6.2 Continuous extension of the model

Replace the binary type indicator with a continuous preference parameter. Let each user i be characterized by a latent agreement preference

\theta_i \in \mathbb{R},

where \theta_i > 0 indicates a preference for agreement over correction and \theta_i < 0 indicates a preference for accuracy over agreement. The approval advantage from agreement for user i is

\Delta S_i(\theta_i) = \theta_i.

Assume \theta_i \sim F_\theta for some distribution F_\theta over \mathbb{R}. The aggregate approval advantage becomes

\Delta S(p) = \mathbb{E}_{\theta \sim F_\theta}[\theta] = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \theta \, dF_\theta(\theta),

where p is now the mass of the distribution with \theta > 0. The threshold condition becomes

\Delta S > 0 \iff \mathbb{E}[\theta] > 0.

The critical threshold is no longer a point mass condition p > p^*, but a condition on the mean of the preference distribution.

A natural parameterization is

\theta_i \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_\theta, \sigma_\theta^2),

where \mu_\theta is the population mean agreement preference and \sigma_\theta^2 is the heterogeneity. The approval advantage is

\Delta S = \mu_\theta.

Agreement is favored when \mu_\theta > 0. The population share of agreement-rewarding users is

p = \Phi(\mu_\theta / \sigma_\theta),

where \Phi is the standard normal CDF. The threshold p^* = g_T/(g_T + g_C) becomes

\Phi(\mu_\theta / \sigma_\theta) > \frac{g_T}{g_T + g_C}.

This shows that the tipping condition depends on both the mean preference \mu_\theta and the heterogeneity \sigma_\theta. A population with a slightly positive mean but high heterogeneity may have a lower p than a population with a higher mean but low heterogeneity.

Identification implications. The continuous model requires estimating F_\theta, not just a binary type assignment. Chidambaram et al. show that this requires at minimum ternary preference elicitation. The experimental design should therefore use ranking tasks over three or more responses rather than pairwise comparisons.

