7. Connection to the RLHF Amplification Mechanism

The threshold p^* = g_T/(g_T + g_C) is not an arbitrary construction. It is the population-level manifestation of a mechanism now formally characterized in the alignment literature.

Shapira, Benade, and Procaccia (2026) present a formal analysis of how RLHF amplifies sycophancy. They identify an explicit amplification mechanism that causally links optimization against a learned reward to bias in the human preference data used for alignment. The direction of behavioral drift is determined by a covariance under the base policy between endorsing the belief signal in the prompt and the learned reward, and the first-order effect reduces to a simple mean-gap condition: if human preference data assigns higher reward to responses that agree with the user than to responses that correct the user, then stronger optimization amplifies agreement-seeking behavior.

The present model's g_C is the user-level analogue of this reward gap; g_T is the countervailing preference for accuracy. The threshold p^* therefore represents the population share at which the aggregate reward gap vanishes.

The population-weighted approval gap \Delta S(p) = p g_C - (1-p) g_T is the aggregate analogue of the mean-gap condition in Shapira et al. The mapping is:

Shapira et al. This framework
Reward gap on a single prompt Approval gap g_C, g_T
Mean-gap condition \Delta S(p) > 0
Population of prompts Population of users
Agreement penalty Correction to g_C or g_T

The closed-form agreement penalty derived by Shapira et al. can be re-expressed as a function of the estimated population parameters. If the mean-gap condition holds, the minimal reward correction required to prevent sycophancy from increasing is

\Delta r = -\Delta S(p) = (1-p)g_T - p g_C.

This penalty is negative when \Delta S(p) > 0, i.e., when agreement is favored by the approval signal. The penalty becomes more negative as p increases or as g_C increases relative to g_T.

