10. Learning Dynamics

10.1 The learning rule

Consider a simple approval-learning rule:

x_{t+1} = x_t + \alpha F(\Delta S(p_t), x_t),

where \alpha > 0 is the learning rate. Under a bounded-response approximation:

x_{t+1} = x_t + \alpha x_t(1 - x_t)\Delta S(p_t).

Then the sign of the drift is determined by \Delta S(p_t). Thus:

p_t > p^* \Rightarrow x_t \uparrow,
\qquad
p_t < p^* \Rightarrow x_t \downarrow.

The learning rate \alpha determines the speed of movement but, under appropriate stability conditions, not the direction determined by the sign of \Delta S.

10.2 Sampling bias

The population parameter p need not equal the proportion observed in the assistant's feedback data. Suppose type C users provide feedback at rate r_C and type T users provide feedback at rate r_T. Then the effective fraction of type C observations in the training signal is

\tilde{p} = \frac{pr_C}{pr_C + (1 - p)r_T}.

If r_C > r_T, then \tilde{p} > p. The assistant therefore behaves as if agreement-seeking users were more prevalent than they actually are. The effective tipping condition becomes \tilde{p} > p^*, and the true population threshold can be lower than the naive threshold inferred from an unbiased model.

