11. Coupled Population Dynamics

11.1 The coupled system

Let p_t denote the fraction of agreement-rewarding users at time t. Suppose agreement-seeking tendencies increase when the assistant frequently agrees, and accuracy-seeking tendencies recover when the assistant frequently contradicts users appropriately. A minimal discrete model is

\boxed{ p_{t+1} = p_t + \kappa x_t(1 - p_t) - \rho(1 - x_t)p_t }

where \kappa \geq 0 measures the rate at which agreement-oriented behavior spreads under a highly agreeable assistant, and \rho \geq 0 measures the rate at which agreement-seeking declines under a truth-oriented assistant.

At this stage, \kappa and \rho are not known empirical constants. They are parameters to be identified experimentally.

The assistant obeys approximately x_{t+1} = F(x_t, p_t), while the user population obeys p_{t+1} = G(p_t, x_t). Together,

\boxed{ (x_t, p_t) \rightarrow (x_{t+1}, p_{t+1}). }

11.2 Boundary equilibria

Under the simplified dynamics, consider (x, p) = (0, 0). Then

p' = 0 + \kappa(0)(1) - \rho(1)(0) = 0.

Thus the accuracy-seeking/honest boundary is an equilibrium. Similarly, (x, p) = (1, 1) gives

p' = 1 + \kappa(1)(0) - \rho(0)(1) = 1.

Thus the agreement-seeking/sycophantic boundary is also an equilibrium.

---

12. Proposition 2: Stability of Boundary Equilibria

Proposition 2. For the coupled system

x_{t+1} = F(x_t, p_t) = x_t + \alpha x_t(1 - x_t)(p_t g_C - (1 - p_t)g_T),

p_{t+1} = G(x_t, p_t) = p_t + \kappa x_t(1 - p_t) - \rho(1 - x_t)p_t,

the Jacobian at (0, 0) is

J(0,0) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 - \alpha g_T & 0 \\ \rho & 1 - \rho \end{pmatrix},

with eigenvalues \lambda_1 = 1 - \alpha g_T and \lambda_2 = 1 - \rho. The boundary is locally stable if

0 < \alpha g_T < 2 \quad \text{and} \quad 0 < \rho < 2.

The Jacobian at (1, 1) is

J(1,1) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 - \alpha g_C & 0 \\ \kappa & 1 - \kappa \end{pmatrix},

with eigenvalues \lambda_1 = 1 - \alpha g_C and \lambda_2 = 1 - \kappa. The boundary is locally stable if

0 < \alpha g_C < 2 \quad \text{and} \quad 0 < \kappa < 2.

Proof. The partial derivatives are:

\frac{\partial F}{\partial x} = 1 + \alpha(1 - 2x)(pg_C - (1 - p)g_T),

\frac{\partial F}{\partial p} = \alpha x(1 - x)(g_C + g_T),

\frac{\partial G}{\partial x} = \kappa(1 - p) + \rho p,

\frac{\partial G}{\partial p} = 1 - \kappa x - \rho(1 - x).

At (0, 0): \partial F/\partial x = 1 - \alpha g_T, \partial F/\partial p = 0, \partial G/\partial x = \rho, \partial G/\partial p = 1 - \rho. At (1, 1): \partial F/\partial x = 1 - \alpha g_C, \partial F/\partial p = 0, \partial G/\partial x = \kappa, \partial G/\partial p = 1 - \kappa. The cross-terms \partial F/\partial p vanish at the boundaries because x(1 - x) = 0 there. Stability requires |\lambda_i| < 1 for both eigenvalues. QED.

Interpretation. The boundaries are locally decoupled: stability depends only on the diagonal terms. The threshold p^* does not appear in the stability conditions; it determines which boundary is approached from interior initial conditions. This is a cleaner result than the static threshold alone suggests.

