13. Finite-Population Stochastic Dynamics

13.1 The deterministic model as an approximation

The coupled system is deterministic. It describes the expected trajectory of a finite population under stochastic approximation. The actual population is finite, and demographic noise can cause the system to cross the threshold p^* even when the deterministic dynamics would not.

13.2 Finite-population stochastic model

Let N be the population size. At each time step, a random subset of users is sampled to provide feedback. The stochastic dynamics can be modeled as a Markov chain on the state space \{(n_C, n_T) : n_C + n_T = N\}, where n_C is the number of agreement-rewarding users and n_T is the number of accuracy-rewarding users.

The transition rates are:

T_{n \to n+1} = \kappa x \left(1 - \frac{n}{N}\right) \frac{n}{N} + \kappa x \left(1 - \frac{n}{N}\right)^2,

T_{n \to n-1} = \rho (1-x) \frac{n}{N} \left(1 - \frac{n}{N}\right) + \rho (1-x) \left(\frac{n}{N}\right)^2,

where n = n_C is the number of agreement-rewarding users. The first term represents the spread of agreement-seeking behavior under an agreeable assistant; the second represents the recovery of accuracy-seeking behavior under a truth-oriented assistant.

This is a variant of the Moran process, a fundamental model in evolutionary dynamics. The fixation probability of a single agreement-rewarding mutant introduced into a population of accuracy-rewarding users is

\phi_1 = \frac{1 - r^{-1}}{1 - r^{-N}},

where r = T_{n \to n+1} / T_{n \to n-1} is the relative fitness of agreement-rewarding behavior. When r > 1, agreement-rewarding behavior has a higher fixation probability than neutral drift.

13.3 The role of stochasticity

The stochastic model reveals three effects absent from the deterministic analysis.

First, fixation probability: even when the deterministic threshold p^* favors accuracy, demographic noise can cause agreement-seeking behavior to fixate in the population. The probability of this event depends on N, \kappa, \rho, and the initial condition.

Second, absorption time: the expected time to reach either (0,0) or (1,1) is finite but can be very long. The population may remain in a meta-stable state near p^* for an extended period.

Third, the critical population size: there exists a population size N^* below which stochastic effects dominate and the deterministic threshold loses predictive power. For N < N^*, the system behaves as a finite-population Markov chain rather than a deterministic dynamical system.

