14. Delayed Feedback as an Intervention

14.1 Combined reward

Suppose the assistant receives only immediate approval. Then the effective objective is approximately J_{\text{approval}} = E[S_0]. Now introduce delayed outcome information:

Y_\tau = B_\tau + \epsilon.

The learning signal becomes

R = \omega S_0 + (1 - \omega)Y_\tau.

With \Delta B = B_T - B_A > 0, agreement has a benefit disadvantage of -\Delta B. The agreement advantage is therefore

\Delta R(p) = \omega \Delta S(p) - (1 - \omega)\Delta B.

Agreement is favored when

\omega[pg_C - (1 - p)g_T] - (1 - \omega)\Delta B > 0.

Solving for p:

\boxed{ p > \frac{\omega g_T + (1 - \omega)\Delta B}{\omega(g_C + g_T)}. }

When \omega = 1, this reduces to p^* = g_T/(g_T + g_C). As delayed benefit information enters, the tipping threshold rises by a term proportional to \Delta B in the numerator. The intervention works by making agreement less attractive in the combined reward signal, not by changing the approval signal itself.

14.2 Independent quality signals

A second intervention is to provide the learner with an independent quality signal Q. For example: independently verified factual accuracy, externally scored task performance, expert evaluation, or calibrated uncertainty. The combined reward might be

R = S_0 + \lambda Q.

If Q is negatively correlated with unjustified agreement and positively correlated with truthful responses, increasing \lambda can increase the effective value of g_T and/or reduce the effective value of g_C. In the minimal model this shifts

p^* = \frac{g_T}{g_T + g_C}.

Interventions can therefore be understood geometrically as moving the tipping boundary.

