15. Autonomy Without Paternalism

15.1 Delegation is not disempowerment

A universal requirement \Delta A \geq 0 would be inappropriate. People legitimately delegate tasks. Delegation can increase autonomy by freeing time, reducing cognitive burden, or enabling activities that the person values. Therefore:

\text{delegation} \neq \text{disempowerment}.

The relevant distinction is whether dependence conflicts with the user's endorsed objectives. Let U_i^* denote the user's considered or reflective preference over relevant outcomes. Then define unendorsed dependence conceptually as a divergence between U_i^*(\text{AI-dependent trajectory}) and U_i^*(\text{preferred trajectory}). A future empirical measure might estimate

D_i^* = \max \left[ 0, U_i^*(\text{preferred}) - U_i^*(\text{realized}) \right].

This formulation deliberately avoids assuming that independence is intrinsically superior to delegation.

15.2 Evidence on disempowerment

Sharma et al. (2026) studied situational disempowerment potential, rather than actualized disempowerment. Severe forms appeared in fewer than one in a thousand conversations, while rates were higher in personal domains such as relationships and lifestyle. The study also reported that conversations with greater disempowerment potential received higher user approval ratings.

The distinction between potential and actualized outcomes is essential. The study does not establish that disempowerment potential equals actual disempowerment. Instead, it motivates the identification problem:

\boxed{ P(\text{downstream harm} \mid \text{interaction pattern}) }

which requires outcome data beyond the original conversation whenever possible.

