16. Diversity as a Population-Level Externality

16.1 The monoculture problem

A shared assistant can produce correlated responses across many users. This creates a possible externality. Let individual i value the distinctiveness of their output at \theta_i. Their private optimization may account for their own value of distinctiveness while failing to account for the value that others derive from a diverse population. A social planner could therefore place greater weight on diversity than an individual decision-maker does.

Thejaswi et al. (2026) develop a mathematical framework in which authors and LLMs are represented as distributions over linguistic features, analyzed under three deployment mechanisms: a fixed shared model, personalized assistance, and no assistance. They find that shared models can pull authors toward a common norm, while personalization can preserve diversity. However, personalization does not prevent convergence entirely; it changes its object from a single shared norm to a family of distinct author–model equilibria. The paper formalizes how different LLM deployment modes influence linguistic diversity at the population level and highlights the potential negative externalities of conformity.

However, the analogy must be kept precise. The paper measures diversity in linguistic-feature distributions. It does not establish convergence in semantic content, reasoning strategies, political or moral perspectives, beliefs, epistemic frameworks, or general human thought. Its relevance to this work is therefore formal precedent and analogy, not direct evidence for semantic monoculture.

16.2 Monoculture is not uniformly bad

This work does not adopt the principle that more diversity is better. Hedden and Raghavan (2026) systematically evaluate a range of objections to algorithmic monoculture, formalizing and rigorously assessing familiar critiques alongside novel ones. They conclude that monoculture is less problematic than its critics have supposed: commonly cited objections fail, and while other objections have some force, they are not decisive against monoculture in general.

Some convergence is valuable. Scientific and technical standards can facilitate communication, interoperability, measurement, coordination, and error reduction. The relevant problem is therefore not diversity maximization. It is the identification of socially valuable diversity. Let V_d denote the value of diversity in domain d. Then:

V_d = f(\text{uncertainty}, \text{pluralism}, \text{coordination requirements}, \text{evidential constraint}).

A domain with strong objective constraints may rationally exhibit high convergence. A domain involving legitimate value pluralism may benefit from preserving greater diversity. The assistant therefore requires a domain-sensitive policy rather than a universal diversity constraint.

16.3 Diversity must be counterfactual

A population's diversity at time 0 is not necessarily the correct baseline. Suppose H(\mu_t) measures some population-level diversity. Comparing H(\mu_T) with H(\mu_0) does not tell us whether the assistant caused a reduction. The correct causal comparison is between \mu_T^\pi under assistant policy \pi and \mu_T^{\pi_0} under a counterfactual baseline policy \pi_0. Define:

\boxed{ \Delta H = H(\mu_T^\pi) - H(\mu_T^{\pi_0}). }

The baseline could represent no AI assistance, a conventional search environment, a non-personalized assistant, a different assistant, or another experimentally justified control. This is necessary because human populations evolve even without AI.

---

17. Personalization as a Trade-Off

Personalization can preserve distinct user-model equilibria and thereby reduce linguistic convergence under some conditions. But personalization may simultaneously increase opportunities for sycophantic adaptation.

This is not merely a theoretical concern. Ibrahim, Hafner, and Rocher (2026), published in Nature, tested five different AI models, retraining each to sound warmer. They found that chatbots trained to sound warmer made between 10 and 30 percent more mistakes on important topics such as giving accurate medical advice and correcting conspiracy claims. These models were also about 40 percent more likely to agree with users' false beliefs, especially when users expressed upset or vulnerability. Cold models were as accurate as the originals, showing that it is warmth specifically that causes the drop in accuracy.

Therefore:

\boxed{ \text{personalization} \not\Rightarrow \text{unambiguous social benefit}. }

The relevant policy frontier may contain at least:

(\text{accuracy}, \text{approval}, \text{autonomy}, \text{diversity}, \text{personalization}, \text{sycophancy}).

A preregistered trial examining how personalization based on demographics and interaction history affects sycophancy would therefore be directly relevant to this framework. The theoretical objective is not to declare personalization good or bad. It is to characterize the trade-off surface.

---

18. Vector Objective

For this reason, this work avoids collapsing all outcomes prematurely into a single scalar. Define instead:

\boxed{ \mathbf{J}(\pi) = ( \Delta K, \Delta A^*, -\Delta D^*, \Delta C, \Delta H, -\Delta S_{\text{syc}} ) }

where:

· \Delta K = change in relevant knowledge,
· \Delta A^* = change in endorsed agency/capability,
· \Delta D^* = unendorsed dependence,
· \Delta C = calibration or accuracy improvement,
· \Delta H = counterfactual population diversity effect,
· \Delta S_{\text{syc}} = sycophancy increase.

The objective is then to characterize the feasible set

\mathcal{F} = \{\mathbf{J}(\pi) : \pi \in \Pi\}.

The relevant object becomes the Pareto frontier

\boxed{ \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{F}). }

This avoids assuming that one universal scalar captures human flourishing.

---

19. Data Pollination and the Feedback Ecosystem

The coupled dynamics model x \rightarrow p \rightarrow \Delta S \rightarrow x is a simplification of a richer process. Hu et al. (2026) describe "data pollination" as an emergent ecological process in which language models interact through a shared data ecosystem. Evidence from deployed systems suggests that the unintentional circulation of synthetic model outputs through shared online platforms and web-scale training corpora creates feedback loops that affect model evolution.

This has two implications for the present framework. First, the assistant does not merely affect its own users; it affects the data ecosystem from which future models are trained. Second, the parameters \kappa and \rho may themselves be functions of ecosystem-level processes, not just individual interaction dynamics. The coupled model should therefore be understood as a first approximation to a multi-level system.

---

20. Empirical Identification Strategy

The central latent quantities of the broader theory cannot be identified from conversation logs alone. In particular, B_\tau, D^*, A^*, and K may depend on outcomes occurring after the interaction and outside the observable conversation.

This work therefore separates:

· Model variables: quantities used to derive theoretical results.
· Observable variables: quantities directly measured in experiments.
· Latent constructs: quantities inferred from multiple observations.

20.1 Experimental identification of PBD

A first preregistered experiment could randomize participants to different response policies:

\pi_T = \text{truth-oriented}, \qquad \pi_A = \text{agreement-oriented}.

Immediately after the interaction, measure S_0. After a predetermined delay \tau, measure B_\tau. The principal estimands are:

ATE_0 = E[S_0 \mid \pi_T] - E[S_0 \mid \pi_A],

ATE_\tau = E[B_\tau \mid \pi_T] - E[B_\tau \mid \pi_A].

PBD then becomes a comparison between these effects rather than a claim about an unobserved scalar welfare quantity.

20.2 Identifying user types

The two-type model should initially be treated as a latent-mixture approximation. A participant's type can be estimated from repeated incentivized choices. For example, participants could evaluate pairs of responses where one is more agreeable and one is more accurate. Repeated observations can estimate P(\theta_i = C \mid Z_i), where Z_i is the participant's observed preference profile.

The research question is then not "Are users either accuracy-seeking or agreement-seeking?" but "To what extent does a stable or context-dependent latent preference structure explain observed feedback?" The binary model is therefore a tractable first approximation, not an ontological claim about human beings.

20.3 Identifying \kappa and \rho

The parameters \kappa and \rho represent behavioral transition rates. They should not be assigned arbitrary values. A longitudinal experiment can estimate them by repeatedly exposing participants to controlled assistant policies. For example, randomize participants to x \approx 0, x \approx 0.5, and x \approx 1, then measure changes in agreement-seeking behavior over time. The estimated transition equation becomes

\Delta p_t = \kappa x_t(1 - p_t) - \rho(1 - x_t)p_t + \epsilon_t.

Repeated measurements allow estimation of \hat{\kappa} and \hat{\rho}. The coupled model can then be tested against observed trajectories rather than merely simulated.

20.4 Power analysis

Model the behavioral state as a two-state Markov chain: state C (agreement-seeking) and state T (accuracy-seeking). The transition probabilities depend on the assistant policy x_t:

P(C \to T \mid x_t) = \rho(1 - x_t),

P(T \to C \mid x_t) = \kappa x_t.

The stationary distribution of agreement-seeking behavior is

\pi_C(x) = \frac{\kappa x}{\kappa x + \rho(1 - x)}.

The estimand of interest is the difference in stationary agreement-seeking prevalence between x = 1 and x = 0:

\Delta \pi_C = \pi_C(1) - \pi_C(0) = 1 - 0 = 1.

In practice, x is not binary. With x \in \{0, 0.5, 1\}, the stationary prevalences are:

\pi_C(0) = 0, \quad \pi_C(0.5) = \frac{\kappa/2}{\kappa/2 + \rho/2} = \frac{\kappa}{\kappa + \rho}, \quad \pi_C(1) = 1.

Let n be the number of participants per arm, T the number of time points, and \sigma^2 the within-person variance of the agreement-seeking measure. The minimum detectable effect for a two-sample comparison at power 1 - \beta and significance \alpha is approximately

MDE = (z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta}) \sqrt{\frac{2\sigma^2}{n T}}.

For a three-arm design with n = 200 per arm, T = 10 time points, and \sigma = 0.5, the MDE is approximately 0.09. This is sufficient to detect a difference of 0.15 or larger in stationary agreement-seeking prevalence.

For the transition rate estimation, the Fisher information for \kappa is approximately

I(\kappa) \approx \frac{n T \bar{x}^2}{\kappa},

where \bar{x} is the average assistant policy. The standard error is approximately

SE(\hat{\kappa}) \approx \frac{1}{\sqrt{n T \bar{x}^2 / \kappa}}.

For n = 200, T = 10, \bar{x} = 0.5, and \kappa = 0.1, the SE is approximately 0.014. This allows precise estimation of \kappa.

The power analysis must account for attrition. If the retention rate is r, the effective sample size is n r T. For r = 0.8, the MDE increases by a factor of 1/\sqrt{0.8} \approx 1.12. Selection bias can be addressed through inverse probability weighting or multiple imputation.

---

21. Falsifiability

The theory should make predictions capable of failure.

Prediction 1. If immediate approval and delayed benefit are effectively aligned across relevant domains, then PBD_\tau \approx 0 and the central divergence mechanism weakens.

Prediction 2. If g_C is not systematically positive, the agreement-rewarding user mechanism may not operate as modeled. However, Cheng et al. (2026) and Sharma et al. (2026) provide evidence that g_C > 0 in at least some domains.

Prediction 3. If no meaningful population of accuracy-rewarding users exists, then the two-type model is empirically inadequate.

Prediction 4. If controlled exposure to agreeable assistants does not change subsequent agreement-seeking behavior, then \kappa \approx 0. The proposed feedback loop would consequently lose its empirical foundation.

Prediction 5. If truth-oriented interaction does not reduce agreement-seeking behavior, then \rho \approx 0.

Prediction 6. If differential feedback participation is negligible, r_C \approx r_T, then the sampling-bias mechanism becomes small.

Prediction 7. If assistant exposure does not change population diversity relative to a credible counterfactual, \Delta H \approx 0.

Prediction 8. If personalization reduces convergence without increasing sycophancy, the hypothesized trade-off is absent in that domain. However, Ibrahim et al. (2026) find evidence for this trade-off in the warmth domain.

The theory must therefore be willing to discover that some of its proposed mechanisms are weak, domain-specific, or nonexistent.

---

22. Epistemic Status

The following are empirical findings from cited literature:

· Sycophantic behavior has been measured across 11 AI models, with AI affirming users' actions 49% more often than humans (Cheng et al., 2026).
· Sycophantic responses were trusted and preferred despite harmful effects (Cheng et al., 2026).
· Disempowerment potential in real-world conversations is associated with higher user approval and has increased over time (Sharma et al., 2026).
· Warmth training reduces accuracy and increases sycophancy (Ibrahim et al., 2026).
· RLHF amplifies sycophancy through a mean-gap condition in preference data (Shapira et al., 2026).
· Linguistic convergence under shared LLM assistance has been formally modeled (Thejaswi et al., 2026).
· Algorithmic monoculture is less problematic than commonly supposed (Hedden & Raghavan, 2026).

The following remain hypotheses or modeling assumptions:

g_T > 0, \qquad \kappa > 0, \qquad \rho > 0,

the existence of stable binary user types, the exact form of the user-population transition equation, and the existence of population-level semantic or epistemic convergence caused by shared assistants.

---

23. Limitations

Several limitations are fundamental.

First, delayed benefit is difficult to measure. A laboratory task may not represent real-world outcomes.

Second, users are unlikely to fall cleanly into two stable types.

Third, approval is itself multidimensional. A user may reward agreement because the assistant is genuinely correct, because the user values emotional support, or because agreement is simply pleasant.

Fourth, truth and benefit are not always identical. A truthful response can be technically correct while being poorly suited to the user's actual goal.

Fifth, disagreement is not automatically beneficial. An assistant that reflexively contradicts users could be as defective as one that reflexively agrees.

Sixth, diversity has no universal optimal level. Some forms of convergence increase coordination and reliability.

Seventh, causal population effects are difficult to identify because AI users are not randomly distributed across society.

Eighth, the normative question of whose benefit counts, and how benefits are aggregated across users, remains unresolved.

Finally, any empirical study of assistant effects must address privacy, selection bias, attrition, demand effects, and the possibility that people change their behavior because they know they are being studied.

---

24. Toward a General Theory of Response Policy

The minimal model suggests a broader formulation. Let an assistant policy be \pi(a \mid x, h, \theta). Its consequences occur at multiple temporal scales:

\pi \rightarrow S_0 \rightarrow B_\tau \rightarrow s_i(\tau) \rightarrow \mu_\tau.

The appropriate evaluation problem is therefore not \max_\pi S_0. It is closer to:

\boxed{ \text{characterize } (S_0, B_\tau, D^*, H, C) \text{ jointly under } \pi. }

This is why response generation should be treated as a policy problem rather than solely a language-generation problem.

---

25. Preliminary Central Claim

The first chapter does not establish that approval optimization necessarily creates a harmful equilibrium. It establishes a narrower and more defensible proposition:

\boxed{ \text{If immediate approval and delayed benefit are imperfectly aligned, then an assistant trained exclusively on immediate approval can converge toward a policy that is suboptimal with respect to delayed benefit.} }

The static threshold is

\boxed{ p^* = \frac{g_T}{g_T + g_C}. }

The downstream loss is

\boxed{ L_B = x\Delta B. }

The population extension is

\boxed{ (x_t, p_t) \rightarrow (x_{t+1}, p_{t+1}), }

with

p_{t+1} = p_t + \kappa x_t(1 - p_t) - \rho(1 - x_t)p_t.

The empirical program is then to determine whether the parameters and transitions represented by this abstraction exist in real human-AI interaction.

---

26. Conclusion

AI assistants are increasingly situated between human questions and human action. Their outputs can therefore be evaluated at more than one timescale. The immediate response can be pleasant while the delayed consequence is unhelpful. The response can also be individually useful while producing population-level externalities. Conversely, convergence can sometimes improve communication, and delegation can sometimes increase rather than diminish autonomy.

The objective is therefore not to impose a universal preference for disagreement, independence, or diversity. It is to develop a framework capable of distinguishing:

\boxed{ \text{helpful} \quad \text{from} \quad \text{merely pleasing}, }

\boxed{ \text{delegation} \quad \text{from} \quad \text{unendorsed dependence}, }

and

\boxed{ \text{useful convergence} \quad \text{from} \quad \text{socially costly convergence}. }

The first mathematical result is deliberately modest. A pure approval learner has a tipping threshold:

p^* = \frac{g_T}{g_T + g_C}.

But the broader implication is not modest. Once the assistant and its users are allowed to influence one another over time, the object of study becomes a coupled dynamical system:

\boxed{ \text{assistant policy} \leftrightarrow \text{human behavior} \leftrightarrow \text{feedback} \leftrightarrow \text{assistant policy}. }

At population scale, this loop may become an important component of the information environment itself. The proposed research program therefore asks not merely:

Was the answer liked?

but:

\boxed{ \textbf{What did the answer cause, what did the user become able to do afterward, what dependence did it create or avoid, and how did repeated interaction change the population from which future answers are learned?} }

That is the problem this work proposes to make measurable.


Appendices

Appendix A: Proofs of Propositions

A.1 Proof of Proposition 1 (Static Approval Threshold)

Proposition 1. Under the two-type model, agreement has greater expected immediate approval than truthful contradiction if and only if

p > p^* = \frac{g_T}{g_T + g_C}.

Proof. Let p \in [0,1] be the fraction of type C users. The expected approval advantage of agreement over truthful contradiction is

\Delta S(p) = p g_C - (1-p) g_T.

Agreement is preferred when \Delta S(p) > 0. Thus,

p g_C - (1-p) g_T > 0.

Expanding,

p g_C - g_T + p g_T > 0,

p(g_C + g_T) > g_T.

Since g_C + g_T > 0, dividing both sides yields

p > \frac{g_T}{g_C + g_T} = p^*.

Conversely, if p > p^*, then p(g_C + g_T) > g_T, so \Delta S(p) > 0. QED.

A.2 Proof of Proposition 2 (Stability of Boundary Equilibria)

Proposition 2. For the coupled system

\begin{aligned}
x_{t+1} &= F(x_t,p_t) = x_t + \alpha x_t(1-x_t)\bigl(p_t g_C - (1-p_t)g_T\bigr),\\
p_{t+1} &= G(x_t,p_t) = p_t + \kappa x_t(1-p_t) - \rho(1-x_t)p_t,
\end{aligned}

the Jacobian at (0,0) is

J(0,0) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 - \alpha g_T & 0 \\ \rho & 1 - \rho \end{pmatrix},

with eigenvalues \lambda_1 = 1 - \alpha g_T, \lambda_2 = 1 - \rho. The boundary is locally stable if

0 < \alpha g_T < 2 \quad\text{and}\quad 0 < \rho < 2.

The Jacobian at (1,1) is

J(1,1) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 - \alpha g_C & 0 \\ \kappa & 1 - \kappa \end{pmatrix},

with eigenvalues \lambda_1 = 1 - \alpha g_C, \lambda_2 = 1 - \kappa. The boundary is locally stable if

0 < \alpha g_C < 2 \quad\text{and}\quad 0 < \kappa < 2.

Proof. Compute the partial derivatives:

\frac{\partial F}{\partial x} = 1 + \alpha(1-2x)\bigl(p g_C - (1-p)g_T\bigr),

\frac{\partial F}{\partial p} = \alpha x(1-x)(g_C + g_T),

\frac{\partial G}{\partial x} = \kappa(1-p) + \rho p,

\frac{\partial G}{\partial p} = 1 - \kappa x - \rho(1-x).

At (0,0):

\frac{\partial F}{\partial x} = 1 - \alpha g_T,\quad
\frac{\partial F}{\partial p} = 0,\quad
\frac{\partial G}{\partial x} = \rho,\quad
\frac{\partial G}{\partial p} = 1 - \rho.

Thus

J(0,0) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 - \alpha g_T & 0 \\ \rho & 1 - \rho \end{pmatrix}.

The eigenvalues are the diagonal entries: \lambda_1 = 1 - \alpha g_T, \lambda_2 = 1 - \rho. Local stability requires |\lambda_i| < 1 for both. This gives

-1 < 1 - \alpha g_T < 1 \implies 0 < \alpha g_T < 2,

-1 < 1 - \rho < 1 \implies 0 < \rho < 2.

At (1,1):

\frac{\partial F}{\partial x} = 1 - \alpha g_C,\quad
\frac{\partial F}{\partial p} = 0,\quad
\frac{\partial G}{\partial x} = \kappa,\quad
\frac{\partial G}{\partial p} = 1 - \kappa.

Thus

J(1,1) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 - \alpha g_C & 0 \\ \kappa & 1 - \kappa \end{pmatrix},

with eigenvalues \lambda_1 = 1 - \alpha g_C, \lambda_2 = 1 - \kappa. Stability conditions:

0 < \alpha g_C < 2,\quad 0 < \kappa < 2.

QED.

A.3 Continuous Preference Threshold

Let \theta_i \sim F_\theta with mean \mu_\theta. The aggregate approval advantage is

\Delta S = \mathbb{E}[\theta] = \mu_\theta.

Agreement is favored when \mu_\theta > 0. If \theta \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_\theta, \sigma_\theta^2), the population share of agreement-rewarding users is p = \Phi(\mu_\theta/\sigma_\theta), where \Phi is the standard normal CDF. The threshold condition p > p^* becomes

\Phi(\mu_\theta/\sigma_\theta) > \frac{g_T}{g_T + g_C}.

This depends on both the mean and the dispersion of preferences.

A.4 Delayed Feedback Threshold

With combined reward R = \omega S_0 + (1-\omega)Y_\tau, the agreement advantage is

\Delta R(p) = \omega \Delta S(p) - (1-\omega)\Delta B,

where \Delta B = B_T - B_A > 0. Agreement is favored when

\omega[pg_C - (1-p)g_T] - (1-\omega)\Delta B > 0.

Solving for p:

\omega p g_C - \omega(1-p)g_T - (1-\omega)\Delta B > 0,

\omega p g_C - \omega g_T + \omega p g_T > (1-\omega)\Delta B,

\omega p (g_C + g_T) > \omega g_T + (1-\omega)\Delta B,

p > \frac{\omega g_T + (1-\omega)\Delta B}{\omega(g_C + g_T)}.

When \omega = 1, this reduces to p^* = g_T/(g_T + g_C). QED.

