Appendix B: Continuous Preference Distribution Details

B.1 Identifiability

Chidambaram et al. (AISTATS 2026) prove that binary comparisons are insufficient for identifying latent user preferences from finite user data and infinite users. Even incomplete rankings over three or more responses ensure identifiability. Therefore, experimental elicitation must use ternary or higher-arity ranking tasks.

B.2 Normal Distribution Example

Assume \theta_i \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_\theta, \sigma_\theta^2). The approval advantage is \Delta S = \mu_\theta. The population share of agreement-rewarding users is

p = \Phi\left(\frac{\mu_\theta}{\sigma_\theta}\right).

The threshold condition p > p^* becomes

\Phi\left(\frac{\mu_\theta}{\sigma_\theta}\right) > \frac{g_T}{g_T + g_C}.

Solving for \mu_\theta:

\mu_\theta > \sigma_\theta \Phi^{-1}\left(\frac{g_T}{g_T + g_C}\right).

This shows that a higher heterogeneity \sigma_\theta requires a higher mean \mu_\theta to cross the threshold, because a larger fraction of the distribution must be above zero.

B.3 General Distribution

For any distribution F_\theta with mean \mu_\theta, the condition is simply \mu_\theta > 0. The threshold p^* is a derived quantity:

p^* = F_\theta(0),

the probability mass below zero. The condition p > p^* is equivalent to \mu_\theta > 0 only if F_\theta is symmetric. In general, p and \mu_\theta are not one-to-one.

---

Appendix C: Finite-Population Moran Process

C.1 Transition Rates

Let n be the number of agreement-rewarding users, N the total population. The transition rates are

T_{n \to n+1} = \kappa x \left(1 - \frac{n}{N}\right) \frac{n}{N} + \kappa x \left(1 - \frac{n}{N}\right)^2,

T_{n \to n-1} = \rho (1-x) \frac{n}{N} \left(1 - \frac{n}{N}\right) + \rho (1-x) \left(\frac{n}{N}\right)^2.

The first term in each expression represents the probability that an agreement-seeking user influences an accuracy-seeking user (or vice versa). The second term represents the probability that two users of the same type interact and one converts the other.

C.2 Fixation Probability

For a single agreement-rewarding mutant in a population of accuracy-rewarding users, the fixation probability is

\phi_1 = \frac{1 - r^{-1}}{1 - r^{-N}},

where r = T_{n \to n+1} / T_{n \to n-1} is the relative fitness. When r > 1, agreement-seeking behavior has a higher fixation probability than neutral drift. When r < 1, it is likely to be eliminated.

C.3 Absorption Time

The expected absorption time starting from n agreement-rewarding users satisfies the recurrence

\tau_n = 1 + T_{n \to n+1} \tau_{n+1} + T_{n \to n-1} \tau_{n-1} + (1 - T_{n \to n+1} - T_{n \to n-1}) \tau_n,

with boundary conditions \tau_0 = \tau_N = 0. Solving this recurrence gives the expected time to reach either boundary. In general, \tau_n is maximized near n = N/2 and scales exponentially with N when the system is near neutral.

C.4 Critical Population Size

There exists a population size N^* below which stochastic effects dominate and the deterministic threshold loses predictive power. For N < N^*, the system behaves as a finite-population Markov chain. An approximation for N^* is

N^* \approx \frac{1}{\kappa + \rho} \cdot \frac{1}{\min(p^*, 1-p^*)}.

When N \gg N^*, the deterministic approximation is accurate. When N \ll N^*, the stochastic model must be used.

---

Appendix D: Sampling Bias Correction

Let r_C and r_T be the feedback rates for type C and type T users. The effective fraction of type C in the training signal is

\tilde{p} = \frac{p r_C}{p r_C + (1-p) r_T}.

If r_C > r_T, then \tilde{p} > p. The assistant behaves as if agreement-seeking users were more prevalent. The effective tipping condition is \tilde{p} > p^*. Solving for the true population share p:

\frac{p r_C}{p r_C + (1-p) r_T} > p^*.

Cross-multiplying:

p r_C > p^* [p r_C + (1-p) r_T],

p r_C (1 - p^*) > p^* (1-p) r_T,

p \left[ r_C (1 - p^*) + p^* r_T \right] > p^* r_T,

p > \frac{p^* r_T}{r_C (1 - p^*) + p^* r_T}.

This is the true population threshold. When r_C > r_T, this threshold is lower than p^*. For example, if p^* = 0.5, r_C = 0.8, r_T = 0.2, then

p > \frac{0.5 \cdot 0.2}{0.8 \cdot 0.5 + 0.5 \cdot 0.2} = \frac{0.1}{0.4 + 0.1} = 0.2.

So the true threshold is 0.2, not 0.5.

---

Appendix E: Domain-Specific Welfare Threshold

For a domain d, let \Delta B_d = B_{T,d} - B_{A,d} > 0. The welfare-optimal threshold is derived by equating the welfare gain from agreement to the welfare loss:

p g_C - (1-p)g_T - \Delta B_d = 0.

Solving for p:

p(g_C + g_T) = g_T + \Delta B_d,

p^*_W = \frac{g_T + \Delta B_d}{g_T + g_C + \Delta B_d}.

When \Delta B_d > 0, p^*_W > p^*. The gap p^*_W - p^* measures the domain-specific welfare cost of pure approval optimization.

