Appendix I: Robustness and Sensitivity

I.1 Sensitivity to g_T, g_C

The threshold p^* = g_T/(g_T + g_C) is sensitive to the ratio g_T/g_C. Small changes in g_T or g_C can shift p^* substantially.

g_T g_C p^*
0.2 0.8 0.20
0.4 0.6 0.40
0.5 0.5 0.50
0.6 0.4 0.60
0.8 0.2 0.80

I.2 Sensitivity to \kappa, \rho

The stability conditions 0 < \kappa < 2 and 0 < \rho < 2 are broad. The system is stable for most reasonable values. However, the speed of convergence depends on \kappa, \rho. If \kappa and \rho are both small, the system may remain near p^* for a long time.

I.3 Sensitivity to Initial Conditions

The basin of attraction of (0,0) and (1,1) depends on p^*. If p_0 > p^*, the system moves toward (1,1). If p_0 < p^*, it moves toward (0,0). When p_0 = p^*, the system is at the boundary and may be sensitive to noise.

I.4 Alternative Functional Forms

The bounded-response learning rule x_{t+1} = x_t + \alpha x_t(1-x_t)\Delta S is one of many possible forms. Alternatives include:

· Linear: x_{t+1} = x_t + \alpha \Delta S
· Sigmoid: x_{t+1} = \sigma(\sigma^{-1}(x_t) + \alpha \Delta S)
· Exponential: x_{t+1} = x_t \exp(\alpha \Delta S)

The qualitative results are robust to the choice of functional form, but the quantitative dynamics differ.

